  <Patch Id="Ug0QuOquTVqz0s5kSJORLr">
    <Canvas Id="H6HXN6DL56eE7Wbhw5kowU" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="Q9vrTF6gcq1p0mfUnEEBPv">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="H4Dsel9uXqYEz8OLInBz5Z">
        <Canvas Id="Ps5nHmi4szCHCZmQO4y4pa" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="HeoBd4zDnGM3ovEsuOcWmb" Bounds="-820,-140,640,780" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="ACT IV LAB - Experiment: read only what you need. NOT a shipped unit.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The contradiction this experiment exists for: a big remote file, of which a question needs a&#xD;&#xA;sliver. Can a patch read the SLIVER - by byte address - instead of the file?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE FILE: a public PMTiles archive of Stamen's watercolor world map, 18,588,472 bytes, sitting on&#xD;&#xA;a web server. This patch never downloads it. FLIP FETCH: exactly TWO requests go out, each with an&#xD;&#xA;HTTP Range header - the thing this experiment was built to test, because vvvv's HTTPGet has a&#xD;&#xA;Headers pin nobody in this course had ever wired.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;REQUEST 1 asks for bytes 0-126: the archive's 127-byte header, which names where everything else&#xD;&#xA;lives. Watch HEADER BYTES read 127 - not 18 million. The server answered 206 Partial Content:&#xD;&#xA;'here is the part you asked for'.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;REQUEST 2 asks for bytes 66585-80015: one watercolor tile (z4, the Alps), 13,431 bytes, 0.07% of&#xD;&#xA;the archive - and DRAWS it. The bytes arrive, ImageDecoder eats them directly (this archive stores&#xD;&#xA;tiles uncompressed), and the picture on the right is real, fetched from the middle of a file by&#xD;&#xA;street address.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE THE ADDRESS CAME FROM, honestly: the archive's directory - read and parsed by&#xD;&#xA;lab\probe-pmtiles.py (127-byte header -&gt; 3 KB root directory -&gt; this tile's offset), NOT by this&#xD;&#xA;patch. The claim proven here is 'a remote file can be read by address'; parsing varint directories&#xD;&#xA;is bookkeeping the lab script does and records. One wrong byte in that parsing cost a JPEG its&#xD;&#xA;first ff - the finding is in the script's header.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHY IT MATTERS: this is the entire mechanism of cloud-native GIS. COG, PMTiles, GeoParquet,&#xD;&#xA;Zarr - all of them are ordinary files on ordinary servers, ARRANGED so that a directory read&#xD;&#xA;tells you which bytes answer your question. No tile server, no API - just HTTP asking for a&#xD;&#xA;byte range. The two Explanations' lessons meet here: WHERE you read (the layout) and HOW MUCH&#xD;&#xA;(the pyramid) become addresses, and data access becomes the last box of the pipeline diagram.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Rules of the lab: no new library surface (HTTPGet's Headers pin shipped all along), fetch only on&#xD;&#xA;the toggle, results as numbers in docs/ACT-IV-LAB.md. Data: Map tiles by Stamen Design (CC BY 3.0),&#xD;&#xA;archived by Cooper Hewitt / maplibre demotiles.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="LLJLufcHf5IffPSJEL0yDT" Comment="FETCH" Bounds="60,0,70,35" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="False">
